{
  "profile": "ecdsa-p256-deterministic/1",
  "description": "ECDSA P-256 signatures as an issuer emits them: SHA3-256 prehash, an RFC 6979 deterministic nonce, low-s, raw r||s. One key over one message yields exactly one signature, so two implementations that sign the same base_certificate with the same key emit the same bytes.",
  "rules": {
    "curve": "P-256 (secp256r1). n is the group order, G the base point.",
    "prehash": "h = SHA3-256(message), 32 bytes. The message is the exact byte string signed; for a composite signature it is M' (see composite-m-prime). It is hashed once.",
    "nonce": "k per RFC 6979 section 3.2 with H = SHA3-256: HMAC-SHA3-256 throughout, qlen = 256, x = the private scalar as 32 big-endian bytes, h1 = h, bits2octets(h1) = (int_be(h1) mod n) as 32 bytes, no additional data. The first candidate k in [1, n-1] for which r and s are non-zero is used.",
    "signature": "r = x(k*G) mod n; s = k^-1 * (int_be(h) + r*x) mod n.",
    "low_s": "If s > n/2 (n >> 1), s is replaced by n - s. expected_low_s_applied records whether that happened; the nonce is unchanged either way.",
    "encoding": "raw-rs-64: r || s, 32 bytes each, big-endian. Not DER. In a composite the component is {alg: \"ECDSA-P256-SHA3-256\", encoding: \"raw-rs-64\", low_s: true, sig_b64} with standard base64 and padding.",
    "keys": "Each key names its source. issuer-key-derivation: the scalar of that family from master_seed_hex. key-derivation: the P-256 scalar of that family from seed_hex. scalar: scalar_hex as given. scalar_hex is always written out so an implementation can sign without deriving.",
    "not_the_hmac_hash": "The HMAC hash is SHA3-256, the hash applied to the message. A signer that derives k with HMAC-SHA-256 over the same SHA3-256 prehash is deterministic too, and its signatures verify, but its bytes are not these. A signer with a random nonce likewise verifies and does not match."
  },
  "why_this_is_a_primitive": "Every verifier accepts any valid low-s signature, so a divergence here never fails a verification. What it breaks is reproducibility: the same issuer, key and certificate produce different bytes in different implementations, and a test that compares two emissions can only check that each verifies. Pinning the nonce makes the signature itself comparable.",
